│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ icosahedron_plus_antipodal_optimization                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ A good configuration for 14 points in 3D maximizing min/max distance is close to a scaled │
│                          │ icosahedron-type arrangement plus additional points, but a strong known heuristic is to   │
│                          │ take points on a sphere arranged like vertices of an icosahedron (12 points) plus 2 polar │
│                          │ points, then locally optimize by projected gradient ascent on the squared ratio. To keep  │
│                          │ it robust and fast, I implement: (1) initialize with 12 icosahedron vertices normalized   │
│                          │ plus 2 points near the poles, (2) run a few hundred iterations of a simple                │
│                          │ repulsion-style optimization on the unit sphere (gradient ascent on min-distance proxy),  │
│                          │ and (3) return the resulting points. The repulsion optimization with power-law kernels is │
│                          │ a well-established method for spherical codes (Tammes problem), which directly improves   │
│                          │ the min/max ratio since after normalization all distances ≤ 2 and maximizing the minimal  │
│                          │ pairwise distance maximizes the ratio. For n=14 on the sphere the known optimal spherical │
│                          │ code has minimal angle ~57.14°, giving squared ratio ≈ (2 sin(28.57°))²/4 ≈ 0.228, which  │
│                          │ yields combined_score ≈ 0.228 * 4.166 ≈ 0.95. This is a massive improvement over the      │
│                          │ random baseline (0.02).                                                                   │
