│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_start_direct_ratio_optimization                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current program optimizes a spherical code (all points on the unit sphere), but the   │
│                          │ actual objective is dmin/dmax, which is not necessarily maximized by a spherical          │
│                          │ configuration — the best known 14-point configurations achieve ratio ≈ 0.49, while        │
│                          │ spherical codes only reach ≈ 0.446. Instead, optimize the ratio directly: at each step    │
│                          │ compute pairwise distances, rescale the configuration so its diameter (dmax) equals 1,    │
│                          │ and apply strong short-range repulsion (power-law weights emphasizing the closest pairs,  │
│                          │ which drives dmin up). Track the configuration with the best actual dmin/dmax ratio. Add  │
│                          │ multiple random restarts with fixed seeds (plus the icosahedron-based init) to escape bad │
│                          │ local optima. Scaling by dmax each iteration keeps the objective equivalent to the true   │
│                          │ ratio, and evaluating the ratio on the rescaled points is scale-invariant, so we directly │
│                          │ maximize the score. This should lift the ratio from ~0.46 toward ~0.49 (score > 0.95).    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
