│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ softmin_ratio_optimizer                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current force-based relaxation keeps all points on a unit sphere, which caps the      │
│                          │ achievable ratio: the diameter of the configuration stays 2 while the best packing of 14  │
│                          │ points on a sphere only reaches dmin ≈ 0.59, giving ratio² ≈ 0.09 at best, far from the   │
│                          │ reference 0.24. The true optimum of dmin/dmax is scale/rotation/translation invariant and │
│                          │ generally does NOT lie on a sphere (the optimal configuration can have a smaller diameter │
│                          │ relative to its minimum separation). So instead of a sphere-constrained relaxation, I     │
│                          │ directly optimize a smooth approximation of the target ratio (dmin/dmax)² over freely     │
│                          │ moving points, using L-BFGS with many random restarts. Smooth softmin/softmax over        │
│                          │ pairwise distances give useful gradients; after each run the exact ratio is computed and  │
│                          │ the best configuration kept. Random restarts make it robust; fixed seeds keep it          │
│                          │ deterministic. Points are normalized (centered, max coordinate scaled) at the end only as │
│                          │ a convenience — this doesn't affect the score.                                            │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
