│ patch_name               │ structured_inits_and_two_phase_restarts                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach relies purely on random spherical starts, which rarely land in the   │
│                          │ basin of the optimal 14-point arrangement (known squared ratio ≈ 1/4.1658 ≈ 0.24003). For │
│                          │ N=14 on/in a sphere, good configurations are highly structured (e.g., an icosahedral      │
│                          │ 12-point shell plus 2 poles, or hexagonal-bipyramid-like arrangements). Two changes:      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Add structured initial guesses (icosahedron vertices + 2 poles, cube+octahedron+poles, │
│                          │ anti-prism based) as candidate starts in addition to random ones — these start much       │
│                          │ closer to the optimum and converge to better local optima.                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. Use a two-phase restart budget per the recommendation: first pass with cheap maxiter   │
│                          │ to rank candidates, then fully converge only the top few. This keeps runtime near the     │
│                          │ current 1.2s while exploring more starts.                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should push the squared ratio from ~0.21 toward ~0.24, raising combined_score from   │
│                          │ 0.89 toward 1.0.                                                                          │
