│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ slsqp_hybrid_optimizer                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current coordinate-descent optimizer is both slow (166s) and slightly less accurate   │
│                          │ than the earlier SLSQP variant (0.96). Replace it with a fast hybrid pipeline: (1) cheap  │
│                          │ repulsion pre-optimization that normalizes the diameter to 1 and pushes close pairs       │
│                          │ apart, (2) SLSQP polish that maximizes dmin subject to dmax <= 1, (3) a repeated SLSQP    │
│                          │ re-polish loop that re-normalizes and re-optimizes until the ratio stops improving (SLSQP │
│                          │ can stall at shallow minima, and restarting from its own output often gains digits). Use  │
│                          │ diverse structured seeds (icosahedron + perturbed poles, Fibonacci sphere with several    │
│                          │ offsets, random directions). This mirrors the approach that already achieved 0.96 while   │
│                          │ adding the restart-polish loop and more seeds to squeeze out further gains, and runs in   │
│                          │ ~20-30s instead of 166s.                                                                  │
