│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bottleneck_perturb_polish                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The SLSQP polish maximizes the non-smooth dmin and often stalls because its gradient only │
│                          │ sees the single bottleneck pair. Following the recommendation, I add a cheap micro-search │
│                          │ stage applied to the top-2 candidates: identify the point involved in the smallest        │
│                          │ pairwise distance, nudge it a small step along the direction away from its nearest        │
│                          │ neighbor (i.e., along the gradient of that pair's distance), then re-run one short SLSQP  │
│                          │ pass. This directly attacks the binding constraint without disturbing the overall         │
│                          │ configuration. Since dmin/dmax for 14 points in 3D is bounded by a kissing-number style   │
│                          │ argument, small targeted escapes from stalled minima are exactly where gains remain.      │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
