│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ smooth_softmin_and_bottleneck_polish                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current SLSQP maximizes a non-smooth min-distance objective, which stalls easily      │
│                          │ because the gradient only "sees" a single bottleneck pair. Two targeted changes:          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Replace the non-smooth objective with a smooth softmin surrogate, `-(1/β)·log(Σ        │
│                          │ exp(-β·d_k))`, which approximates the true minimum (for β ~ 50–100 the error is           │
│                          │ negligible) but gives informative gradients on all near-minimal pairs. SLSQP handles this │
│                          │ far better and can escape flat/stalled configurations.                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. Add the recommended bottleneck-escape micro-search: after SLSQP converges, identify    │
│                          │ the point participating in the smallest pairwise distance and push it directly away from  │
│                          │ its nearest neighbor (a finite-difference direction of dmin) with a few decreasing step   │
│                          │ sizes, renormalizing diameter each time. Any improvement is fed back into one more SLSQP  │
│                          │ pass. This directly attacks the exact pair limiting the ratio at near-zero extra cost.    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Both changes are applied to every polished candidate so the global best can only improve. │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
