│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ slsqp_input_guard_and_smooth_escalation                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ Two targeted improvements:                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Input guard in `_slsqp_packing`** (the recommended edit): a malformed or non-finite  │
│                          │ candidate coming out of `_opt` (repulsion/L-BFGS stage) would currently crash `minimize`  │
│                          │ and waste all prior work. Adding a cheap validity check at the top (finite, correct       │
│                          │ shape, positive diameter) and returning `None` on failure lets the caller fall back to    │
│                          │ the fallback path gracefully — zero cost, closes the remaining failure window.            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Escalating smooth-softmin objective inside SLSQP**: the highest-scoring prior        │
│                          │ program (combined_score 1.00) used a smooth soft-min objective                            │
│                          │ `-1/beta*log(sum(exp(-beta*d)))` with escalating beta (20 → 60 → 200 → 1000) and diameter │
│                          │ re-normalization between rounds, instead of the hard `min()`. The hard min is nonsmooth:  │
│                          │ its gradient only moves one pair at a time, so SLSQP stalls near ties. The soft-min gives │
│                          │ gradients to *all* close pairs simultaneously, letting ties equalize properly (which is   │
│                          │ exactly what the optimum of this packing problem looks like — many equal minimal          │
│                          │ distances), then sharpens to converge to the true min. Re-normalizing the diameter after  │
│                          │ each round keeps each restart feasible. This was the key ingredient of the 1.00-scoring   │
│                          │ variant and is cheap to reintroduce here.                                                 │
