├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_slsqp_polish_and_better_seeds                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The current code relies on a single smoothed L-BFGS-B pass per random seed, and the       │
│                          │ smooth min/max (softmin/softmax with beta=20) poorly approximates the true ratio near the │
│                          │ optimum, leaving score at 0.93 while the reference is 1.0. Two targeted improvements: (1) │
│                          │ better structured starting guesses (antipodal cube split, two concentric shells, points   │
│                          │ on a sphere perturbed inward), since the optimal 14-point 3D configuration is close to a  │
│                          │ combination of an outer antipodal framework with inner points; (2) add a polishing stage  │
│                          │ that optimizes the *exact* ratio: iteratively rescale so dmax = 1, then use SLSQP to      │
│                          │ maximize the true minimum pairwise distance subject to all pairwise distances <= 1 and    │
│                          │ pairwise distances >= current dmin (a standard "maximize t s.t. dist_ij >= t"             │
│                          │ Tammes-style formulation, which directly raises dmin/dmax). SLSQP handles the non-smooth  │
│                          │ min via a single variable t with inequality constraints. Also raise beta in the smoothed  │
│                          │ objective for a tighter approximation. Validation guards ensure malformed optimizer       │
│                          │ output never crashes the run. These changes directly target the true metric rather than   │
│                          │ its smooth surrogate.                                                                     │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
