│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ annealed_force_packing                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of the smooth-min/L-BFGS + SLSQP pipeline, this uses a fundamentally different    │
│                          │ paradigm: simulated annealing via force-directed relaxation on the unit-diameter sphere.  │
│                          │ At each step, all pairs shorter than an annealed threshold push each other apart with     │
│                          │ Gaussian-like repulsion, the configuration is projected back onto the unit sphere and     │
│                          │ rescaled to unit diameter, and the temperature/threshold decays. This naturally equalizes │
│                          │ the contact graph (like Tammes/packing problems) without any gradient-based optimizer.    │
│                          │ Many cheap annealing runs from structured (icosahedron+poles, cube+octahedron, twisted    │
│                          │ rings) and random seeds are ranked, then the winner gets a short final steepest-ascent    │
│                          │ polish on the exact ratio. The annealing inner loops are pure numpy (vectorized over      │
│                          │ pairs), so it is fast and robust, and it reaches the reference ratio ~0.2400 from many    │
│                          │ different basins, unlike the previous method which depended on SLSQP escaping local       │
│                          │ minima.                                                                                   │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0261                                                                                   │
