│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_antipodal_symmetry_polish                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The best known configurations for 14 points in 3D (the Tammes/packing problem) are        │
│                          │ conjectured/known to have a high degree of symmetry, and one of the most effective        │
│                          │ symmetry reductions is the antipodal one: the 14 points split into 7 pairs {v_i, -v_i}.   │
│                          │ This halves the number of free variables from 42 to 21, removes all translational         │
│                          │ freedom, and dramatically smooths the constrained optimization landscape for SLSQP (fewer │
│                          │ variables, symmetric structure automatically satisfies many constraints with equality, so │
│                          │ the soft-min objective has many tied active pairs and the reduced parameterization lets   │
│                          │ SLSQP balance them jointly rather than coordinate-wise).                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I add an `_antipodal` helper that (a) detects the antipodal pairing in the incumbent, (b) │
│                          │ reparameterizes to 7 free vectors, and (c) runs escalating SLSQP passes on the reduced    │
│                          │ problem under the diameter<=1 constraint, keeping the best feasible result. It is         │
│                          │ inserted as a final polish stage after the incumbent re-polish, guarded with try/except   │
│                          │ and finite checks so it can never degrade the incumbent. Because the metric is squared    │
│                          │ ratio (dmin/dmax)^2, any symmetric configuration achieving a slightly larger dmin         │
│                          │ directly raises combined_score above the current plateau at 1.00. The added cost is small │
│                          │ (7-variable SLSQP passes) so eval time stays within budget.                               │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
