│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ skip_final_polish_when_target_met                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The current program already reaches the reference-quality score of 1.00 (squared ratio ≈  │
│                          │ 0.2400, matching 1/4.1658). Since the objective appears to sit at the known optimum for   │
│                          │ 14 points in 3D, further optimization passes only burn wall time without improving        │
│                          │ combined_score (eval_time is reported but not scored, yet staying far under timeout is    │
│                          │ still safer). The main inefficiency is Stage 4 (incumbent re-polish) and Stage 3, which   │
│                          │ run even after `best >= target` has already been achieved in Stage 2 — Stages 3 already   │
│                          │ guards on `best < target`, but Stage 4 runs unconditionally, re-running two SLSQP chains  │
│                          │ on a configuration that is already converged. I gate Stage 4 on `best < target`, and      │
│                          │ additionally add a very cheap high-sharpness final SLSQP pass (single beta, few           │
│                          │ iterations) that is only executed when the target was met, to squeeze out any tiny        │
│                          │ remaining slack (e.g., the 5th–6th decimal of the ratio) at negligible cost. This keeps   │
│                          │ the score at 1.00 while cutting roughly 10–20 seconds of redundant SLSQP work from the    │
│                          │ ~47s runtime, improving robustness against timeout variance across evaluator machines     │
│                          │ without risking any quality regression.                                                   │
